    "https://williammary.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/WM/job/Posting-Location---William--Mary/Assistant-Professor-of-English--Anglophone-Literatures-of-Modernity-and-Modernism-_JR102068": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of English (Anglophone Literatures of Modernity and Modernism)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-08\nFull time\n\nJob Requisition: JR102068 Assistant Professor of English (Anglophone Literatures of Modernity and Modernism) (Open) Job Posting Title: Assistant Professor of English (Anglophone Literatures of Modernity and Modernism) Department: CC00092 WM001 | CAS | English Dept Job Family: Faculty - Tenure Track (Less than 12 months) Worker Sub-Type: Regular (benefited) Job Requisition Primary Location: Tucker Hall Primary Job Posting Location: Posting Location - William & Mary Job Description Summary: The Department of English at William & Mary, a public university of the Commonwealth of Virginia, seeks applications for a tenure track position at the Assistant Professor level in Anglophone Literatures of Modernity and Modernism. Appointment will begin August 10, 2027. Duties include research, teaching, and service to the University. The applicant is expected to establish and maintain a vibrant research program that inspires a highly motivated undergraduate student body. Teaching expectation is 2 courses per semester. We are particularly interested in individuals with teaching and research expertise that can extend understandings of modernism beyond the traditional U.K.-U.S. transatlantic axis, and who can teach more than one literary genre. Successful applicants will possess the skills to teach compelling courses ranging from introductory and more specialized courses in their field to the gateway course for our major (“Interpreting Literature”) and to early-to-mid-20th century Anglophone literature survey courses, broadly conceived. William & Mary English courses often support W&M’s interdisciplinary general education curriculum (the COLL Curriculum), and candidates interested in contributing to COLL are welcome to apply. Job Description: Required Qualifications: Applicants must hold a Ph.D. in English, Comparative Literature, or a closely related field (with demonstrated graduate study, teaching, and research expertise in Anglophone modernist literatures) at the time appointment begins (August 10, 2027). Applicants must have completed 18 graduate credit hours in the discipline in which they will teach. Evidence of capacity to teach courses that expand understanding of modernism beyond the traditional U.K.-U.S. transatlantic axis. Evidence of capacity to teach more than one literary genre. Evidence of capacity to teach significant texts and issues in U.S. and/or U.K. modernist literatures. Preferred Qualifications: Evidence of scholarly achievement or demonstrated potential as a scholar. Evidence of capacity to teach a range of literary studies courses, from introductory gateway courses to period surveys and specialized topics courses. Previous experience in undergraduate teaching and mentoring. Applicants must apply online at https://go.wm.edu/VphZB3 . All applications must consist of the following five documents, which are all submitted via the “Resume/Coverletter” upload field\" Incomplete applications may not be considered. Each individual file must not exceed a size limit of 5MB. If necessary, candidates can compress multiple files into a single zip file for upload.  Curriculum vitae Cover letter. Append to your letter a statement of teaching interests that describes your approach to teaching and mentoring (this may also incorporate a discussion of how your scholarly work informs your approach) and a statement of research interests. Each statement should be no more than 2 pages. Unofficial graduate transcripts outlining proof of 18 graduate credit hours in the degree program(s) specified in the position description above. For international applicants, a letter of graduate credit hour completion from your graduate school may be submitted if unofficial transcripts are not provided by your university. Two syllabi or course proposals, one for ENGL 353: International Modernism and one for either ENGL 352: Modern British and Irish Literature or ENGL 364: American Modernism to the 1960s. A writing sample of 20-25 pages. Conditions of Employment: This position may require additional hours beyond the typical work week, including occasional overnight and weekend work as needed to meet the business needs of the operation.  Applicants are encouraged to reflect on teaching and mentoring practices that advance academic excellence and success of all students in literary studies in your cover letter, teaching, and/or research statements. Candidates who reach the first-round virtual interview stage will be prompted to submit online the names and email addresses of three references who will be contacted by the system with instructions for how to submit a letter of reference. For full consideration, submit application materials no later than October 9, 2026. Applications received after the initial application deadline will be considered if needed. All required supplemental application materials mentioned above must be clearly identified and included with your application for full consideration.  Information on the degree programs in the Department of English may be found at https://www.wm.edu/as/english/. Information on William & Mary’s general education COLL Curriculum may be found at https://www.wm.edu/as/undergraduate/coll/ . Benefits William & Mary offers our full-time employees a full array of benefits including retirement, health insurance with options for expanded dental and vision along with group and optional life insurance with coverage for spouse and children, flexible spending accounts, and an EAP (Employee Assistance Program).  Our employees enjoy additional university benefits such as educational assistance, professional development, wellness benefits, and a robust holiday schedule. Housing Relocation Resources are also available to help new employees transition successfully to the area. All employees have access to fitness facilities on campus, to the university libraries, and much more. To learn more, go to: https://www.wm.edu/offices/uhr/benefits/index.php?type=none Applicant Notice: By applying for this position, you consent to William & Mary's use, review, and retention of your application materials for recruitment and employment purposes. Materials may be shared with authorized individuals participating in the hiring process and will be managed in accordance with applicable laws and universi ty policies. Additional Job Description: Job Profile: JP0516 - Assistant Professor (Less than 12 months) TE - Exempt - Salary - S99 Qualifications: Compensation Grade: S99 Recruiting Start Date: 2026-09-08 Position Restrictions: Continuing E&G funded position and not term-limited EEO is the Law. Applicants can learn more about William & Mary’s status as an equal opportunity employer by viewing the \"Know Your Rights\" poster published by the U.S. Equal Employment Opportunity Commission. https:// www.eeoc.gov/know-your-rights-workplace-discrimination-illegal   Background Check: William & Mary is committed to providing a safe campus community. W&M conducts background investigations for applicants being considered for employment. Background investigations include reference checks, a criminal history record check, and when appropriate, a financial (credit) report or driving history check. Applicant Notice:   By applying for this position, you consent to William & Mary's use, review, and retention of your application materials for recruitment and employment purposes. Materials may be shared with authorized individuals participating in the hiring process and will be managed in accordance with applicable laws and university policies. Probationary Period Notice: Individuals selected for full-time staff positions will be required to complete a twelve (12) month probationary period in accordance with university policy.   Remote Work Disclaimer: Remote work eligibility is not guaranteed and is subject to approval. Employee eligibility depends on the likelihood of the employee succeeding in a remote work arrangement and the supervisor’s ability to manage remote workers. Departments and/or Human Resources may amend, alter, change, delete, or modify eligibility."
    },
    "https://williammary.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/WM/job/Posting-Location---William--Mary/Assistant-Teaching-Professor-of-Music-Musicology_JR102065": {
      "title": "Assistant Teaching Professor of Music/Musicology",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-08\nFull time\n\nJob Requisition: JR102065 Assistant Teaching Professor of Music/Musicology (Open) Job Posting Title: Assistant Teaching Professor of Music/Musicology Department: CC00087 WM001 | CAS | Music Dept Job Family: Faculty - Non-Tenure Track (Less than 12 months) Worker Sub-Type: Fixed Term (requires end date - benefited) (Fixed Term) Job Requisition Primary Location: Music Arts Center Primary Job Posting Location: Posting Location - William & Mary Job Description Summary: The Department of Music at William & Mary, a public university of the Commonwealth of Virginia, invites applications for a 3-year, non-tenure track Assistant Teaching Professor of Music instructional position that will begin August 10, 2027. This full-time teaching faculty appointment carries a service component commensurate with rank and comes with the possibility of ongoing renewals and a framework for promotion per the Teaching Faculty Framework. We seek an individual with demonstrated experience teaching one-semester survey courses in western music history, courses in music theory along with accompanying musicianship labs, seminars on music research topics, and topics courses that contribute to the departmental curriculum.  The ability to offer a one-semester survey course in global musics, as well as offerings in popular music, and in the music business is preferred, but not required.  The successful candidate will have experience in teaching small writing/research intensive seminars, larger lecture courses, and the ability and interest to develop and maintain professional community networks that support student learning and departmental initiatives. The successful applicant will be expected to be an effective teacher with prior experience leading classes at the college level and will have a [3-3] teaching load. Job Description: Required Qualifications: A Ph.D. degree is required at the time the appointment begins (August 10, 2027).  Applicants must have completed 18 graduate credit hours in the discipline in which they will teach.  Ability to teach existing courses in western music history, music theory (including lab sections), musicology, small writing/research intensive seminars, and other lecture and seminar-style courses that contribute to the departmental curriculum. Evidence of successful prior teaching experience in leading classes at the college level. Ability and interest in developing and maintaining professional community networks that support student learning and departmental initiatives. Preferred Qualifications: Ability to offer a one-semester survey course in global musics, popular music, and especially music business. Conditions of Employment: This position may require additional hours beyond the typical work week, including occasional overnight and weekend work as needed to meet the business needs of the operation. This position is restricted to a specific term. Applicants must apply online at https://go.wm.edu/VphZB3 . All applications must consist of the following materials for full consideration; Please note: No more than 5 individual documents may be uploaded into the upload field provided (“i.e. Resume/Cover letter” upload field). Each individual file must not exceed a size limit of 5MB. If necessary, candidates can compress multiple files into a single zip file for upload: Curriculum vitae,  Cover letter;  A statement of teaching interests that describes your approach to teaching and mentoring and that may also incorporate a discussion of how your past scholarly work informs your approach; and Unofficial graduate transcripts outlining proof of 18 graduate credit hours in the curricular area specified in the position description above. Additional forms of credit hour verification may be submitted if unofficial transcripts are not provided by your university Applicants are encouraged to reflect on teaching and mentoring practices that advance academic excellence and success of all learners/scholars in music in any of the aforementioned required documents. Candidates who reach the first-round virtual interview stage will be prompted to submit online the names and email addresses of three references who will be contacted by the system with instructions for how to submit a letter of reference. For full consideration, submit application materials no later than the review begin date, October 9, 2026. All required supplemental application materials mentioned above must be clearly identified and included with your application for full consideration. Review of applications is ongoing. Applications received after the initial review date will be considered if needed. Information on the degree programs in the Department of Music may be found at https://www.wm.edu/as/music/undergradprogram/ . Benefits William & Mary offers our full-time employees a full array of benefits including retirement, health insurance with options for expanded dental and vision along with group and optional life insurance with coverage for spouse and children, flexible spending accounts, and an EAP (Employee Assistance Program). Our employees enjoy additional university benefits such as educational assistance, professional development, wellness benefits, and a robust holiday schedule. Housing Relocation Resources are also available to help new employees transition successfully to the area. All employees have access to fitness facilities on campus, to the university libraries, and much more. To learn more, go to: https://www.wm.edu/offices/uhr/benefits/index.php?type=none Applicant Notice By applying for this position, you consent to William & Mary's use, review, and retention of your application materials for recruitment and employment purposes. Materials may be shared with authorized individuals participating in the hiring process and will be managed in accordance with applicable laws and university policies. Additional Job Description: Job Profile: JP0515 - Assistant Professor (Less than 12 months) NTE - Exempt - Salary - S99 Qualifications: Compensation Grade: S99 Recruiting Start Date: 2026-09-04 Position Restrictions: Restricted Term EEO is the Law. Applicants can learn more about William & Mary’s status as an equal opportunity employer by viewing the \"Know Your Rights\" poster published by the U.S. Equal Employment Opportunity Commission. https:// www.eeoc.gov/know-your-rights-workplace-discrimination-illegal   Background Check: William & Mary is committed to providing a safe campus community. W&M conducts background investigations for applicants being considered for employment. Background investigations include reference checks, a criminal history record check, and when appropriate, a financial (credit) report or driving history check. Applicant Notice:   By applying for this position, you consent to William & Mary's use, review, and retention of your application materials for recruitment and employment purposes. Materials may be shared with authorized individuals participating in the hiring process and will be managed in accordance with applicable laws and university policies. Probationary Period Notice: Individuals selected for full-time staff positions will be required to complete a twelve (12) month probationary period in accordance with university policy.   Remote Work Disclaimer: Remote work eligibility is not guaranteed and is subject to approval. Employee eligibility depends on the likelihood of the employee succeeding in a remote work arrangement and the supervisor’s ability to manage remote workers. Departments and/or Human Resources may amend, alter, change, delete, or modify eligibility."
