    "https://williammary.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/WM/job/William--Mary/Editor-of-William-and-Mary-Quarterly_JR100984": {
      "title": "Editor of William and Mary Quarterly",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-01\nFull time\n\nJob Requisition: JR100984 Editor of William and Mary Quarterly (Open) Job Posting Title: Editor of William and Mary Quarterly Department: CC00007 WM001 | PRES | OIEAHC - Omohundro Institute Job Family: Faculty - Tenure Track (12 months) Worker Sub-Type: Regular (benefited) Job Requisition Primary Location: William & Mary Primary Job Posting Location: William & Mary Job Description Summary: Job Description Summary The Omohundro Institute of Early American History and Culture invites applications for Editor of the William and Mary Quarterly (WMQ). Since 1892, this globally respected journal has shaped the field through rigorous peer review, influential scholarship, and a commitment to advancing new historical perspectives. The Editor of the WMQ will lead one of the field's most influential publications and help shape the future of historical research and intellectual exchange. The Editor of the WMQ will oversee all aspects of editorial vision, peer review, and production. The role includes close collaboration with authors, reviewers, editorial staff, and the Book Review Editor, as well as leadership in the annual WMQ-EMSI workshops. Beyond the journal, the Editor will play a vital role in the intellectual community of the Omohundro Institute, contributing to a vibrant program of publications, lectures, conferences, and seminars. Jointly sponsored by William & Mary and the Colonial Williamsburg Foundation, the Institute offers an exceptional environment for scholarly engagement and leadership. The successful candidate will possess a Ph.D. in a relevant discipline; a strong record of scholarly publication and achievement in early American studies or a related field; and a demonstrated aptitude for sustained editorial work. Additional responsibilities include teaching one course annually in the candidate's area of expertise, mentoring undergraduate and graduate students, as appropriate, and participating fully in the intellectual and service activities of the home tenure department. The successful candidate will maintain an active and impactful research agenda that engages and inspires both undergraduate students and M.A./Ph.D. students. This is a 12-month, tenure-eligible faculty appointment at the rank of Full Professor or advanced Associate Professor. The initial appointment is for a renewable 10-year term and is expected to begin on June 25, 2027   Job Description: Required Qualifications: Applicants must hold a Ph.D. at the time appointment begins (June 25, 2027). A record of scholarly excellence and a demonstrated commitment to sustained editorial work evidenced by experience editing scholarly volumes, encyclopedias, handbooks, journals, or book series; serving as an editor or co-editor of scholarly publications; and/or contributing to the editorial direction and quality of academic publishing through service on editorial boards or as peer reviewer   Preferred Qualifications: Previous experience in teaching and mentoring successful undergraduate and graduate students. Conditions of Employment: This position is restricted to a specified term.  Applicants must apply online at https://go.wm.edu/VphZB3 . All applications must consist of the following materials for full consideration; (Please note: No more than 5 individual documents may be uploaded into the upload field provided. Each individual file must not exceed a size limit of 5MB. If necessary, candidates can compress multiple files into a single zip file for upload) Cover letter A statement of teaching interests A statement of research interests A reference page with the contact information (including names, email addresses, and phone numbers) for three references. Applicants are encouraged to reflect on teaching and mentoring practices that advance academic excellence and success of all learners/scholars in early American Studies in any of the aforementioned required documents.  For full consideration, submit application materials by the initial review date, November 24 2026. Applications received after the initial review date will be considered if needed. Posting Range: 126,600-154,000 commensurate with experience. Additional Job Description: Benefits Summary Statement William & Mary offers our employees a full array of benefits including retirement, health insurance with options for expanded dental and vision along with group and optional life insurance with coverage for spouse and children, flexible spending accounts, and an EAP (Employee Assistance Program). Background Check Statement ​ William & Mary is committed to providing a safe campus community. W&M conducts background investigations for applicants being considered for employment. Background investigations include reference checks, a criminal history record check, and when appropriate, a financial (credit) report or driving history check. Job Profile: JP0155 - Professor/12-Mth - Exempt - Salary - S99 Qualifications: Compensation Grade: S99 Recruiting Start Date: 2026-09-01 Position Restrictions: Restricted Term EEO is the Law. Applicants can learn more about William & Mary’s status as an equal opportunity employer by viewing the \"Know Your Rights\" poster published by the U.S. Equal Employment Opportunity Commission. https:// www.eeoc.gov/know-your-rights-workplace-discrimination-illegal   Background Check: William & Mary is committed to providing a safe campus community. W&M conducts background investigations for applicants being considered for employment. Background investigations include reference checks, a criminal history record check, and when appropriate, a financial (credit) report or driving history check. Probationary Period Notice: Individuals selected for full-time staff positions will be required to complete a twelve (12) month probationary period in accordance with university policy.   Remote Work Disclaimer: Remote work eligibility is not guaranteed and is subject to approval. Employee eligibility depends on the likelihood of the employee succeeding in a remote work arrangement and the supervisor’s ability to manage remote workers. Departments and/or Human Resources may amend, alter, change, delete, or modify eligibility."
    },
    "https://williammary.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/WM/job/Posting-Location---Washington-Center/XMLNAME--Adjunct-Professor--William---Mary-Washington-Center--DC-Semester-Program--Open-Pool-_JR101957": {
      "title": "Adjunct Professor (William & Mary Washington Center, D.C. Semester Program, Open Pool)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-04\nPart time\n\nJob Requisition: JR101957  Adjunct Professor (William & Mary Washington Center, D.C. Semester Program, Open Pool) (Open) Job Posting Title:  Adjunct Professor (William & Mary Washington Center, D.C. Semester Program, Open Pool) Department: CC00238 WM001 | PROV | Washington DC Semester Job Family: Faculty - Adjunct Worker Sub-Type: Adjunct (non-benefited) Job Requisition Primary Location: Washington Center Primary Job Posting Location: Posting Location - Washington Center Job Description Summary: The College of Arts & Sciences at William & Mary, a public university of the Commonwealth of Virginia, is recruiting a pool of qualified individuals for possible part-time adjunct teaching appointments with the William & Mary Washington Center in Washington, D.C. The College of Arts & Sciences provides academic oversight while adjunct faculty also have a functional reporting relationship with the Senior Associate Director of the Washington Center, who oversees day-to-day work and operations at the Washington Center. This pool will be accessed ONLY when an opening arises. Your credentials will remain active as long as the pool position is available but no more than one year. After one year, if you are still interested, you must reapply to the most current adjunct advertisement. Adjunct faculty teaching in the DC Programs will be responsible for: · Teaching assigned undergraduate courses · Preparing syllabi and course materials in accordance with academic unit, CAS, and university curricular policies and practices · Delivering instruction via modalities designated in the final contract · Providing structured assessments of student learning that correspond to course learning objectives and syllabus policies · Holding office hours · Engage in consistent communication with the Washington Center staff regarding student attendance, participation, and well-being Job Description: Appointment Details:   Appointments are limited to a specified term and may include the following instructional sessions:   Fall Accelerated Session 1: 8/10/26-10/24/26   Fall Accelerated Session 2: 9/25/26- 12/24/26   Fall Traditional Full Semester:  8/10/2026-1/9/2027   Spring Accelerated Session 1: 1/10/27- 3/24/27   Spring Accelerated Session 2: 3/10/27- 5/24/27  Spring Traditional Full Semester:  1/10/2027-5/24/2027  Required Qualifications:   A Master’s degree with a minimum of 18 graduate-level credits in the field of the proposed course of instruction, or equivalent experience and qualifications.  Previous experience teaching at a US higher education institution.   Preferred Qualifications:   A Ph.D. or other appropriate terminal degree, or ABD status, at the time of appointment (August 10 for fall or January 10 for spring).   Experience designing coursework for experiential learning programs Conditions of Employment: This position is also not eligible for work visa sponsorship. This is a restricted part-time position, is restricted to the term in which it is assigned and hired, and is not eligible for benefits. The position may require additional hours beyond the typical work week, including occasional evenings, weekends, or overnight work as needed to meet operational requirements. Hourly employees are subject to the limitations of the Virginia Manpower Control Program. Total hours worked may not exceed an average of 29 hours per week over a twelve-month period beginning May 1 each year. Within Arts & Sciences, adjunct instructional workload limits apply. Total adjunct work hours for part-time employment from May 1 through April 30 may not exceed 1,500 hours, generally equating to up to 14 credit hours for fall/spring and up to 6 for summer, not to exceed 18 credit hours for the year. Adjuncts within Arts & Sciences at William & Mary must maintain an in-person presence for the duration of their appointment.   Applicants must apply online at  https://go.wm.edu/VphZB3 . All applications must consist of the following materials for full consideration; (Please note: No more than 5 individual documents may be uploaded into the upload field provided. Each individual file must not exceed a size limit of 5MB. if necessary, candidates can compress multiple files into individual zip files for upload):  Cover letter specifying the courses that you are qualified to offer and would be interested in teaching. The most successful courses connect content to students’ location for the semester in Washington DC. Courses should meet either the learning objectives of COLL 300 or COLL 350 within William & Mary’s general education curriculum. The cover letter should also suggest which CAS academic unit may host your course and your qualifications to teach within that unit.  Curriculum vitae. Teaching evaluations (if available). A course proposal and syllabus.   Applicants are encouraged to reflect on teaching and mentoring practices that advance academic excellence and success of all learners/scholars in their discipline in any of the aforementioned required documents.      Candidates who are scheduled for interviews will be prompted to submit online the names and email addresses of three references who will be contacted by the system with instructions for how to submit a letter of reference.  For full consideration, submit application materials no later than the initial review date, September 4, 2026.  All required supplemental application materials mentioned above must be clearly identified and included with your application for full consideration.  Applications received after the initial review date will be considered if needed and as additional hiring needs arise.  Additional information about the Washington Center in D.C. may be found at https://www.wm.edu/offices/dccenter/ . Additional Job Description: Job Profile: JP0738 - Adjunct Professor NTE - Exempt - Salary - S99 Qualifications: Compensation Grade: S99 Recruiting Start Date: 2026-07-24 Position Restrictions: Restricted Term EEO is the Law.  Applicants can learn more about William & Mary’s status as an equal opportunity employer by viewing the \"Know Your Rights\" poster published by the U.S. Equal Employment Opportunity Commission. https:// www.eeoc.gov/know-your-rights-workplace-discrimination-illegal   Background Check: William & Mary is committed to providing a safe campus community. W&M conducts background investigations for applicants being considered for employment. Background investigations include reference checks, a criminal history record check, and when appropriate, a financial (credit) report or driving history check.    Remote Work Disclaimer: Remote work eligibility is not guaranteed and is subject to approval. Employee eligibility depends on the likelihood of the employee succeeding in a remote work arrangement and the supervisor’s ability to manage remote workers. Departments and/or Human Resources may amend, alter, change, delete, or modify eligibility."
    },
    "https://williammary.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/WM/job/Posting-Location---William--Mary/Adjunct-Professor-of-Theatre-and-Performance---Stage-Combat-_JR101689": {
      "title": "Adjunct Professor of Theatre and Performance  (Stagecraft, Stage Management, Sound Design, Stage Combat, Ballet Technique & Open Pool)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-07-31\nPart time\n\nJob Requisition: JR101689 Adjunct Professor of Theatre and Performance  (Stagecraft, Stage Management, Sound Design, Stage Combat, Ballet Technique & Open Pool) (Open) Job Posting Title: Adjunct Professor of Theatre and Performance  (Stagecraft, Stage Management, Sound Design, Stage Combat, Ballet Technique & Open Pool) Department: CC00088 WM001 | CAS | Theatre & Performance Job Family: Faculty - Adjunct Worker Sub-Type: Adjunct (non-benefited) Job Requisition Primary Location: Phi Beta Kappa Hall Primary Job Posting Location: Posting Location - William & Mary Job Description Summary: The Department of Theatre and Performance at William & Mary, a public university of the Commonwealth of Virginia, is recruiting a pool of qualified individuals for possible part-time adjunct teaching appointments. For the current academic year, the department anticipates instructional needs in Stagecraft and associated labs, Stage Management, Sound Design, Stage Combat, or Ballet Technique. This pool will remain open to address additional teaching needs that may arise throughout the year. Within the College of Arts & Sciences, adjunct faculty may be responsible for: • Teaching assigned undergraduate and/or graduate courses depending on unit need • Preparing syllabi and course materials • Delivering instruction and assessing student learning • Holding office hours • Fulfilling other instructional duties consistent with departmental needs Your application will remain active as long as the pool position is available but no more than one year. After one year, if you are still interested, you must reapply to the most current adjunct advertisement. Job Description: Required Qualifications: A Master's degree in Theatre or a closely related field, or equivalent experience and qualifications. For Stage Combat Course - Certification in Stage Combat through the Society of American Fight Directors or the equivalent experience and qualifications. (This is requirement is only necessary if you are applying to teach Stage Combat). Preferred Qualifications: Previous academic teaching experience. Conditions of Employment: This position is also not eligible for work visa sponsorship. This is a  part-time position restricted to the term to which it is assigned and hired, and is not eligible for benefits. The position may require additional hours beyond the typical work week, including occasional evenings, weekends, or overnight work as needed to meet operational requirements. As part-time exempt employees, adjuncts are subject to the limitations of the Virginia Manpower Control Program. Total hours worked may not exceed an average of 29 hours per week over a twelve-month period beginning May 1 each year. Within Arts & Sciences, adjunct instructional workload limits apply. Total adjunct work hours for part-time employment from May 1 through April 30 may not exceed 1,500 hours, generally equating to up to 14 credit hours for fall/spring and up to 6 for summer, not to exceed 18 credit hours for the year. Adjuncts within the College of Arts & Sciences at William & Mary must maintain an in-person presence for the duration of their appointment.  Remote teaching opportunities are rare and subject to approval. Appointments are limited to a specified term and may include the following instructional sessions: Summer Session 1: May 26 – June 26* Summer Session 2: June 29 – July 31*  (expected contract effective date: June 25) Summer Session 3: May 26 – July 31* Fall: August 10 – January 9 Spring: January 10 – May 24  * Summer Session Terms are subject to vary each year   Applicants must apply online at  https://go.wm.edu/VphZB3 . All applications must consist of the following materials for full consideration. (Please note: No more than 5 individual documents may be uploaded into the upload field provided. Each individual file must not exceed a size limit of 5MB. If necessary, candidates can compress multiple files into individual zip files for upload): Curriculum vitae; Cover letter; A statement of teaching interests that describes the courses you are qualified and interested in teaching, as well as your approach to teaching and mentoring and that may also incorporate a discussion of how your past scholarly work informs your approach; A reference page with the contact information (including names, email addresses, and phone numbers) for three references. Applicants are encouraged to reflect on teaching and mentoring practices that advance academic excellence and success for all learners/scholars in Theatre & Performance in any of the aforementioned required documents. Candidates who are scheduled for interviews will be prompted to submit online the names and email addresses of three references who will be contacted by the system with instructions for how to submit a letter of reference. For full consideration, submit application materials no later than the initial review date June 11, 2026. All required supplemental application materials mentioned above must be clearly identified and included with your application for full consideration.  Applications received after the initial review date will be considered if needed and as additional hiring needs arise. Information on the degree programs in the Department of Theatre and Performance may be found at   https://www.wm.edu/as/theatre-performance/ . Additional Job Description: Job Profile: JP0738 - Adjunct Professor NTE - Exempt - Salary - S99 Qualifications: Compensation Grade: S99 Recruiting Start Date: 2026-05-11 Position Restrictions: Restricted Term EEO is the Law.  Applicants can learn more about William & Mary’s status as an equal opportunity employer by viewing the \"Know Your Rights\" poster published by the U.S. Equal Employment Opportunity Commission. https:// www.eeoc.gov/know-your-rights-workplace-discrimination-illegal   Background Check: William & Mary is committed to providing a safe campus community. W&M conducts background investigations for applicants being considered for employment. Background investigations include reference checks, a criminal history record check, and when appropriate, a financial (credit) report or driving history check.    Remote Work Disclaimer: Remote work eligibility is not guaranteed and is subject to approval. Employee eligibility depends on the likelihood of the employee succeeding in a remote work arrangement and the supervisor’s ability to manage remote workers. Departments and/or Human Resources may amend, alter, change, delete, or modify eligibility."
